  "articleSummary": "The article, \"No Fate But What We Bill,\" argues that the agentic AI threat comes not from a single breakthrough but from two compounding curves: endurance (how long one agent can work autonomously, citing METR's time-horizon doubling every ~4–7 months) and breadth (how many sub-agents one orchestrator can coordinate, citing Anthropic's disclosure of GTG-1002, where Claude Code orchestrated espionage against roughly thirty targets). Its key points are that attackers steal access rather than buy capability, so the victim pays the bill (the \"LLMjacking\" pattern described by Sysdig), and that price and access gates fail because they filter buyers, not tenants; it also argues that attackers can succeed through sheer numbers of cheap attempts, since 1−(1−p)^n rises quickly. The intended audience is security and engineering teams who dismiss agentic threats as hype. The tone is analytical and persuasive, with a punchy, somewhat wry voice, and it relies on a recurring framing device drawn from *The Terminator*: Kyle Reese's \"absolutely will not stop\" line, reinterpreted as \"absolutely will not stop billing,\" with Skynet as a distributed system and the robot as merely an API call. Technical terms to keep consistent include \"orchestrator,\" \"sub-agent,\" \"LLMjacking,\" \"time-horizon,\" and \"swarm.\"",
